### Before

In `ngp`, `insertSignalDebugNames` mutates `MagicString` via `s.appendLeft(...)`, whereas `sliceGuarded` sliced the unmutated `s.original` string and `buildInputsMap` / `compileNgModuleDef` sliced raw `content: string`. Because decorator expressions (such as `@Injectable.useFactory`, `@Component.providers`, `@Input.transform`, and `@NgModule.providers`) are re-emitted into `ɵprov`, `ɵcmp`, `ɵdir`, `ɵinj`, and `ɵsetClassMetadata` via `sliceGuarded`, any `signal(...)` / `computed(...)` / `linkedSignal(...)` call inside a decorator argument lost its implicit `debugName`:

```ts
features: [
  i0.ɵɵProvidersFeature([
    {
      provide: TOKEN,
      useFactory: () => {
        const providerSignal = signal(42);
        return { s: providerSignal };
      },
    },
  ]),
],
```

### After

`insertSignalDebugNames` records its `SourceInsertion` entries per `MagicString` instance, and `sliceGuarded` splices both `FORWARD_REFERENCE_GUARD` and any in-span signal `debugName` insertions when slicing decorator spans, matching `ngtsc` (where `signalMetadataTransform` runs on the `ts.SourceFile` after `ivyTransformFactory`):

```ts
features: [
  i0.ɵɵProvidersFeature([
    {
      provide: TOKEN,
      useFactory: () => {
        const providerSignal = signal(
          42,
          ...((ngDevMode
            ? [{ debugName: 'providerSignal' }]
            : /* istanbul ignore next */ []) as []),
        );
        return { s: providerSignal };
      },
    },
  ]),
],
```

### Before

In `packages/compiler-cli/preprocessor`, `getProjectRelativePath` only checked a single `ctx.rootDir` string, and `getHmrFilePath` returned the first matching `rootDirs` entry rather than the most specific (longest-prefix) match. When `rootDirs` has nested or sibling entries (such as a workspace root `.` alongside `src`, `extra`, or `bazel-out/.../bin`), or when `rootDir` is unset and only `rootDirs` is configured, `ɵsetClassDebugInfo`, `ɵɵattachSourceLocations` (`relativeTemplatePath`), and HMR component IDs emitted either `null` or a less-specific relative path than `ngtsc`:

```ts
i0.ɵɵattachSourceLocations('extra/app/app.component.html', [[0, 0, 0, 0]]);
// ...
i0.ɵsetClassDebugInfo(AppComponent, {
  className: 'AppComponent',
  filePath: 'src/app/app.component.ts',
  lineNumber: 9,
});
```

### After

`getProjectRelativePath` iterates over all configured `ctx.rootDirs` (falling back to `[ctx.rootDir]`) and returns the shortest non-`..` relative path, matching `ngtsc`'s longest-prefix `rootDirs` sorting in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/file_system/src/path.ts`. `HybridCompiler` also resolves relative `rootDirs` entries against `dirname(tsconfigPath)` when `rootDir` is omitted:

```ts
i0.ɵɵattachSourceLocations('app/app.component.html', [[0, 0, 0, 0]]);
// ...
i0.ɵsetClassDebugInfo(AppComponent, {
  className: 'AppComponent',
  filePath: 'app/app.component.ts',
  lineNumber: 9,
});
```

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

compiler-cli, compiler

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

A `@defer` block never projects its content into a specific `ng-content` slot. Unlike `@if`, `@for`, `@switch` and `@boundary`, it doesn't copy the tag name and attributes of its root node, so the content goes to the default slot even when there's a single root node. This is a known limitation (#58110, tracked in #64504), and wrapping the block in `<ng-container ngProjectAs="...">` works around it.

The problem is that nothing tells you about it. NG8011 (`controlFlowPreventingContentProjection`) warns when an `@if` prevents a node from reaching its slot, but it doesn't look at `@defer` blocks, so the node silently ends up in the wrong place, or isn't rendered at all if the component has no default slot.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  template: `
    <p>title: [<ng-content select="[card-title]" />]</p>
    <p>body: [<ng-content />]</p>
  `,
})
export class Card {}
```

```html
<app-card>
  @defer (on immediate) {
    <b card-title>Title</b>
  } @placeholder {
    <i card-title>Loading title</i>
  }
</app-card>
```

Both the placeholder and the loaded content show up in `body: [...]`. With `@if (true)` in place of `@defer`, the title goes to `title: [...]`.

`TcbControlFlowContentProjectionOp.findPotentialControlFlowNodes` (`packages/compiler/src/typecheck/ops/content_projection.ts`) only collects `@if`, `@for`, `@switch` and `@boundary` blocks. Since a `@defer` block never projects into a specific slot, every root node that matches a non-wildcard selector is a real problem, whatever the number of root nodes, and the message could point to `ngProjectAs` instead of the single-root-node advice.

#71398 has the fix and tests.

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No hosted reproduction, but this is self-contained (Angular 22.2.1, `ng build`):

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  template: `
    <p>title: [<ng-content select="[card-title]" />]</p>
    <p>body: [<ng-content />]</p>
  `,
})
export class Card {}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [Card],
  template: `
    <app-card>
      @if (true) {
        <b card-title>Title</b>
      }
    </app-card>

    <app-card>
      @defer (on immediate) {
        <b card-title>Title</b>
      } @placeholder {
        <i card-title>Loading title</i>
      }
    </app-card>
  `,
})
export class App {}

bootstrapApplication(App);
```

The build reports nothing. The page shows:

```
title: [Title]
body: []

title: []
body: [Title]
```

